---
title: "Squid Simulation code"
This R markdown has code for creating simulated data using the squidSim package. It specifically simulates data for a study with four treatment groups (Sch, AM11, SI, Sailfin) where individuals used in the experiment are crossed across all four treatments.This simulation can be used to estimate your ability to detect both fixed and random effects based on your sample size and the type of model you plan to use. For the simulation you must plug in your estimates for effect sizes and variance. I used effect sizes and variances from published studies doing a similar experiment to what I had planned (my study was a replication so it made life easier). You can play around with this; look at the least effect size you would be happy with detecting, increase variance if your data is going to be noisy etc. Simulations are a great planning tool!
```{r}
install.packages("devtools")
devtools::install_github("squidgroup/squidSim")
library(squidSim)
library(devtools)
library(squidSim)
library(lme4)
library(lmerTest)
library(ggplot2)
library(tidyverse)
library(emmeans)
library(ggthemes)
library(ggsignif)
library(ggpubr)
library(MuMIn)
kin_data0 = simulate_population(
#make_structure - tells the function simulate_population how you want your data to be structured. Here, I'm asking it to cross 50 individuals across 4 treatments.
# If you wanted to have a nested data structure rather than crossed (4 treatments with 50 distinct individuals per treatment) -> make_structure("treatment(4)/individuals(50)")
data_structure = make_structure("treatment(4) + individuals(50)", repeat_obs = 1, level_names = list(
treatment = c("Sch","AM11","SI","Sailfin")
)),# repeat_obs -> number of measurements per individual*treatment combination
response_names = "TS",# TS = proportion of time spent in interaction with stimulus fish
n_pop = 1000,#Simulating 1000 data sets with the specified parameters
parameters = list( # Here, you need to have some idea of effect sizes and variance
intercept = 700,
individuals = list(vcov = 0.2),# among individual variance. This generates random intercepts
treatment = list(
fixed = TRUE,# Indicating that you want the treatment effect sizes to be fixed at what you specify rather than being drawn from a distribution
names = c("Sch","AM11","SI","Sailfin"),
beta = c(0,-400,-400,-600) #list of effect sizes. corresponds to the 4 treatments in the same order as the names
),
residual = list(vcov = 0.5) #Specifying residual variance for your response variable
)
)
sim_data = get_population_data(kin_data0, list = TRUE)
sim_data$treatment = factor(sim_data$treatment, levels = c("Sch","AM11","SI","Sailfin"))
# Looking at the spread of the simulated data
hist(sim_data$TS)
sim_data$TS
sim_data = get_population_data(kin_data0, list = TRUE)
sim_data$treatment = factor(sim_data$treatment, levels = c("Sch","AM11","SI","Sailfin"))
# Looking at the spread of the simulated data
hist(sim_data$TS)
sim_data
sim_data$TS
